#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# inactive

#% text
# `inactive(f)` is an inert head: a call `inactive(f)(x, ...)` stays unevaluated until `activate` turns it into `f(x, ...)`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The arguments of an inactive call are evaluated; only the call itself is kept.

#% item
# `d` of an inactive sum, integral or transform is itself inactive: `d(inactive(sum)(f(k, y), [k, a, b]), y)` is `inactive(sum)(d(f(k, y), y), [k, a, b])` when the bounds do not depend on y.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
inactive(add)(1, 2)
#% output
#= inactive(add)(1, 2)

#% cell
activate(inactive(add)(1, 2))
#% output
#= 3

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# A term without inactive heads is just evaluated by `activate`:

#% cell
activate(1 + 2)
#% output
#= 3

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `activate`, `composition`
